# Default: let git handle text normally.
* text=auto

# This policy file carries exact LF-only rules below. Pin its own checkout bytes so
# those rules remain machine-verifiable when core.autocrlf=true on Windows.
.gitattributes text eol=lf

# Go sources keep LF in the working tree on every platform. gofmt normalises line
# endings as part of formatting, so a CRLF checkout makes it report every Go file as
# unformatted -- 793 of them on Windows -- and makes the pre-commit gofmt rewrite files
# nobody edited. The blobs are already LF; this only pins how they are written out.
*.go text eol=lf

# internal/config/lockdigest.go hashes archetype and facet sources byte-for-byte and
# compares the result against the sha256 .standards.lock pins. .standards.lock is
# generated against the LF blobs committed here, so a Windows checkout that converts
# them to CRLF (the windows-latest runner's git default) makes every profile and facet
# fail with "lockfile digest does not match the archetype source" before the file's
# content is ever examined -- confirmed on framework.yaml, whose LF sha256 matches the
# pinned digest exactly and whose CRLF-converted sha256 does not. This was most of the
# Windows leg's failures across cmd/standardsctl, internal/config and internal/devcontainer
# (#135). Pinning LF here is the same fix as *.go, for the same reason: the hash a
# checkout produces must not depend on which platform did the checking out.
.config/archetypes/**/*.yaml text eol=lf

# HISS-16's canonical AGENTS.md and every vendor target standardsctl compile-context
# generates from it (checks.py's own CONTEXT set) are read and compared byte/line-exact:
# internal/compiler's TestCanonicalAgentsFloorCatchesLoss regex-matches a line in AGENTS.md
# with `(?m)^   standardsctl compile-context$`, which a CRLF checkout fails because the
# trailing \r sits between the last matched character and the multi-line $; cmd/standardsctl's
# TestDispatchCommand_RepositoryDogfood audits AGENTS.md against its compiled targets and
# reports "target CLAUDE.md is out of sync with ../../AGENTS.md" once a Windows checkout
# converts one side's line endings and not (consistently) the other's. Same fix, same reason
# as *.go and the archetype yamls above (#135).
AGENTS.md text eol=lf
CLAUDE.md text eol=lf
.windsurfrules text eol=lf
.github/copilot-instructions.md text eol=lf
.gemini/GEMINI.md text eol=lf
.codex/rules.md text eol=lf
.cursor/rules/**/*.mdc text eol=lf

# internal/devcontainer and cmd/standardsctl both verify every committed .devcontainer/
# file (devcontainer.json, Dockerfile.praetor, the base64 source parts) against a recorded
# bootstrap fingerprint computed over its exact bytes (TestDogfoodingSynthesis,
# TestDispatchCommand_ContextAndDevcontainer); a CRLF checkout changes those bytes and the
# verification reports the bootstrap Dockerfile or configuration differing from its recorded
# inputs for a directory nobody edited. Confirmed against Dockerfile.praetor specifically --
# devcontainer.json alone left it still failing; the base64 parts carry no line terminators
# so this is a no-op for them, but the whole directory is one bootstrap unit (#135).
.devcontainer/* text eol=lf

# The bootstrap source capture (bootstrapSourcePathspec in internal/devcontainer) hashes
# go.mod, go.sum and LICENSE beside the Go sources, and `praetorctl devcontainer freshness`
# compares that digest with the bundle's recorded sourceSHA256. A CRLF checkout of these
# three files would report drift on every Windows tree, so they keep LF like *.go.
go.mod text eol=lf
go.sum text eol=lf
LICENSE text eol=lf

# tools/markdownlint assets are embedded into the adopter binary. Keep Praetor's source
# checkout bytes stable so Go embedding and bootstrap fingerprints cannot vary by platform;
# adopted copies may still use one consistent CRLF style, which audit normalizes.
tools/markdownlint/* text eol=lf

# Every staged .sh is shellchecked and every staged .yml/.yaml is yamllint --strict-ed
# by .config/lefthook/scripts/checks.py, and both reject carriage returns outright:
# shellcheck raises SC1017 ("Literal carriage return") on the shebang line and yamllint's
# new-lines rule reports "wrong new line character: expected \n" at 1:1. Under the default
# `* text=auto` a windows-latest checkout writes CRLF, so the pre-push gate failed on
# .config/lefthook/pre-push/pushed-checks.sh, lefthook.yml and .config/lefthook/praetor.yml
# -- files nobody edited -- and took the harness self-tests down with it, because
# test_hooks.py copies those same checkout bytes into its fixture repositories. A CRLF
# shell script is also not executable by any POSIX shell, git-bash included, so LF here is
# the correct working-tree form on every platform, not just the one the gate runs on.
*.sh text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf

# Every file under templates/ is compiled into the binary with go:embed (templates/embed.go)
# and scaffolded verbatim, so its line endings are the adopter's. Under "* text=auto" alone a
# Windows checkout writes them CRLF: a binary built there ships CRLF bodies, and the tests
# that pin LF content (templates/embed_test.go, internal/flavor/emitted_content_test.go) fail
# on that leg only -- a gate that cannot run on a covered platform is not a passing gate
# (HISS-21). Same fix, same reason as *.go and .devcontainer/* above (#135).
templates/** text eol=lf

# internal/adopt/testdata/emitted holds the hook files adoption renders from templates, byte
# for byte (TestEmittedHookFixturesMatchTheRendering), and make hooks-lint lints exactly these
# bytes with black, flake8 and yamllint. Adoption writes them LF on every platform, so a CRLF
# checkout of the Python fixture would lint text no adopter receives (HISS-21).
internal/adopt/testdata/emitted/** text eol=lf

# internal/adopt/testdata/{evasion,checkpoint,personas} hold the earlier interceptor, checkpoint
# script and persona texts adoption refreshes (priorEvasionHookDigests, priorCheckpointDigests,
# priorPersonaDigests). The tests write these bytes into a repository as they are and derive a
# CRLF checkout with crlfText, so a CRLF checkout of the fixtures would turn every line ending
# into \r\r\n and fail those tests on the Windows leg only (HISS-21). They are .py and .md.prior
# files, which no rule above pins, so each directory is pinned here; gitattributes patterns
# take no brace expansion.
internal/adopt/testdata/evasion/** text eol=lf
internal/adopt/testdata/checkpoint/** text eol=lf
internal/adopt/testdata/personas/** text eol=lf

# internal/astmerge's corpus test splits testdata/probe_corpus.txt into cases on "\n" and
# matches its "--- base" section headers exactly, and FuzzASTMerge seeds from the same
# parse. Under "* text=auto" a Windows checkout writes the corpus CRLF, the first header
# reads "--- base\r", and both tests fail on that leg only (HISS-21). The section bodies are
# Go sources merged byte for byte, so the checkout keeps the committed LF bytes; the test
# also normalises one consistent CRLF style for a checkout that ignores this file.
internal/astmerge/testdata/*.txt text eol=lf

# internal/paperclip/testdata holds harness files an earlier release wrote. PatchPlatform
# must change the platform line alone and keep every other byte, and the tests (paperclip's
# patch_platform_test.go and prior_test.go, adopt's manifest_sources_test.go) compare those
# bytes line by line; a CRLF checkout on Windows adds "\r" to every line and fails the
# comparison on that leg only (HISS-21).
internal/paperclip/testdata/** text eol=lf

# The editors/ reference integrations are generator output: `praetorctl editors reference
# --verify` (make verify-all) and internal/editor's TestReferenceSetMatchesTrackedTree compare
# them byte-exact against the LF text internal/editor renders, so a CRLF checkout would report
# both as drift on Windows only (HISS-21). Same fix, same reason as templates/** above.
editors/neovim/lua/standards.lua text eol=lf
editors/jetbrains/inspectionProfiles/standards.xml text eol=lf

# The CRLF fixtures assert that the line-based matchers are not silenced by a Windows
# checkout. Two attributes are needed and they defend different things.
#
#   -text              stops checkout normalisation from deleting the carriage returns,
#                      which are the entire property these files exist to carry.
#   whitespace=cr-at-eol  stops `git diff --check` from rejecting those same carriage
#                      returns as trailing whitespace. Without it the commit hook refuses
#                      the fixtures, so the rule could never be pinned at all.
.config/hiss/testdata/**/crlf-* -text whitespace=cr-at-eol

# The MkDocs preset's example figure becomes an adopter's docs/figures/ and docs/assets/figures/
# once the preset is copied into a repository root, and its JSON binds the same hashes as the
# figures in the managed block below, so it keeps the same LF rule on every platform.
docs/presets/mkdocs/docs/figures/*.ts text eol=lf
docs/presets/mkdocs/docs/assets/figures/* text eol=lf

# The managed attribute block at the end of this file is what `praetorctl adopt` writes in
# every adopting repository (internal/adopt/gitattributes.go): the DevContainer rule unless
# adoption.decline lists dev-container (rule from Attributes in internal/devcontainer/checkout.go),
# then the figure engine's rules for docs:seo-portal (Attributes in tools/figures/assets.go):
#
#   .devcontainer/* text eol=lf    the rule this file also carries above, for the reason given
#                                  there; adoption keeps an operator's own copy of it (#313).
#   tools/figures/** text eol=lf   tools/figures/assets.go embeds the engine into the adopter
#                                  binary, internal/managedasset's TestShippedTextLedger records
#                                  the digest of every embedded text, and `bundle.mjs --check`
#                                  compares a rebuild of the committed player in dist/ byte for
#                                  byte, so a CRLF checkout would fail all three on Windows only.
#   docs/figures/*.ts, docs/assets/figures/* text eol=lf
#                                  each figure's JSON binds the SHA-256 of its spec, the engine
#                                  files (ENGINE_FILES in tools/figures/core.mjs) and both SVGs.
#   tools/figures/third_party/interfig/upstream/** -text
#                                  the vendored interfig subset is byte-identical to upstream at
#                                  the pin, and vendor.json records the SHA-256 of every file. It
#                                  comes last and wins: with text unset, eol has no effect.

# BEGIN praetor managed attributes (praetorctl adopt)
# praetorctl audit hashes these files; keep their checkout bytes the same on every platform.
.devcontainer/* text eol=lf
tools/figures/** text eol=lf
docs/figures/*.ts text eol=lf
docs/assets/figures/* text eol=lf
tools/figures/third_party/interfig/upstream/** -text
# END praetor managed attributes
